{"name":"Pale Celadon","hex":"#c9cbbe","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pale","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Light in color.","Having a pallor (a light color, especially due to sickness, shock, fright etc.)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To turn pale; to lose colour.","To become insignificant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pale"},{"term":"celadon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pale green colour, possibly tinted with gray.","A pale green Chinese glaze."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a pale green colour tinted with gray."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/celadon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Celadon","blocks":[{"text":"Celadon is a term for pottery denoting both wares glazed in the jade green celadon color, also known as greenware or \"green ware\", and a type of transparent glaze, often with small cracks, that was first used on greenware, but later used on other porcelains. Celadon originated in China, though the term is purely European, and notable kilns such as the Longquan kiln in Zhejiang province are renowned for their celadon glazes. Celadon production later spread to other parts of East Asia, such as Japan and Korea, as well as Southeast Asian countries, such as Thailand and Vietnam. Eventually, European potteries produced some pieces, but it was never a major element there. Finer pieces are in porcelain, but both the color and the glaze can be produced in stoneware and earthenware. Most of the earlier Longquan celadon is on the border of stoneware and porcelain, meeting the Chinese but not the European definitions of porcelain."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Song_Dynasty_Celadon_Vase.jpg/330px-Song_Dynasty_Celadon_Vase.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Celadon","wikidata":"Q1054559"},{"title":"Shades of green","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color green may differ in hue, chroma or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a green or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Shades_of_Green.svg/330px-Shades_of_Green.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_green","wikidata":"Q19847180"},{"title":"Colorfulness","blocks":[{"text":"Colorfulness, chroma and saturation are attributes of perceived color relating to chromatic intensity. As defined formally by the International Commission on Illumination (CIE) they respectively describe three different aspects of chromatic intensity, but the terms are often used loosely and interchangeably in contexts where these aspects are not clearly distinguished. The precise meanings of the terms vary by what other functions they are dependent on."},{"items":["Colorfulness is the \"attribute of a visual perception according to which the perceived color of an area appears to be more or less chromatic \". The colorfulness evoked by an object depends not only on its spectral reflectance but also on the strength of the illumination, and increases with the latter unless the brightness is very high.","Chroma is the \"colorfulness of an area judged as a proportion of the brightness of a similarly illuminated area that appears white or highly transmitting\". As a result, chroma is mostly only dependent on the spectral properties, and as such is seen to describe the object color. It is how different from a grey of the same lightness such an object color appears to be.","Saturation is the \"colorfulness of an area judged in proportion to its brightness\", which in effect is the perceived freedom from whitishness of the light coming from the area. An object with a given spectral reflectance exhibits approximately constant saturation for all levels of illumination, unless the brightness is very high."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Brightness_and_colorfulness.png/330px-Brightness_and_colorfulness.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colorfulness","wikidata":"Q27673"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Boddhisattva_Guanyin%2C_China%2C_Ming_dynasty%2C_1300-1400_AD%2C_porcelain_-_%C3%96stasiatiska_museet%2C_Stockholm_-_DSC09644.JPG/500px-Boddhisattva_Guanyin%2C_China%2C_Ming_dynasty%2C_1300-1400_AD%2C_porcelain_-_%C3%96stasiatiska_museet%2C_Stockholm_-_DSC09644.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Boddhisattva_Guanyin%2C_China%2C_Ming_dynasty%2C_1300-1400_AD%2C_porcelain_-_%C3%96stasiatiska_museet%2C_Stockholm_-_DSC09644.JPG/1280px-Boddhisattva_Guanyin%2C_China%2C_Ming_dynasty%2C_1300-1400_AD%2C_porcelain_-_%C3%96stasiatiska_museet%2C_Stockholm_-_DSC09644.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Boddhisattva_Guanyin%2C_China%2C_Ming_dynasty%2C_1300-1400_AD%2C_porcelain_-_%C3%96stasiatiska_museet%2C_Stockholm_-_DSC09644.JPG/500px-Boddhisattva_Guanyin%2C_China%2C_Ming_dynasty%2C_1300-1400_AD%2C_porcelain_-_%C3%96stasiatiska_museet%2C_Stockholm_-_DSC09644.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ming shrine, the figure left unglazed in the \"biscuit\" state","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABoddhisattva_Guanyin%2C_China%2C_Ming_dynasty%2C_1300-1400_AD%2C_porcelain_-_%C3%96stasiatiska_museet%2C_Stockholm_-_DSC09644.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Gr%C3%A8s_chinois_Guimet_231102.jpg/500px-Gr%C3%A8s_chinois_Gui